Foundation
What Is a Job Description?
A JD is your starting point — not the final truth. It describes the role. But it passes through many hands before it reaches you, and each hand can distort it.

💡
The single most important thing to understand
A JD ≠ the complete picture of what the client needs. It is a document — created by HR, filtered by legal, and often outdated. Your job is to read it critically, not accept it literally.

📄 What a JD Typically Contains
Overview / Summary
What the team does, project context. Often generic — but useful for client background.
Primary Responsibilities
Core daily tasks. If there are 15+ bullets, the JD is inflated. Look for 3 that repeat or are most specific.
Secondary / Ancillary Duties
"Other duties as assigned." This is padding — don't let it drive your sourcing criteria.
Skills & Tools
Usually the most accurate section. Clients actually care about this. Check for version numbers.
Qualifications & Education
Often aspirational. "PhD preferred" often means Master's acceptable. Always clarify.
Legal Disclaimers (EEO etc.)
Boilerplate. Do not source from this section — it adds zero candidate criteria.
🏗️ Who Creates a JD (and Why It Gets Distorted)
Hiring Manager
Writes the original — knows exactly what they need. This version is the most accurate.
HR Department
Reviews for policy compliance. Replaces specific language with generic terms. Accuracy reduces here.
Legal Team
Adds disclaimers and broadens language for DEI / compliance. More generalization.
Committee / Approvers
Adds "nice-to-haves" that become listed as requirements. JD inflates here.
HRIS / VMS System
Exports JD as raw data — formatting is lost. What you receive is often garbled output.
YOU — The Recruiter
Reconstruct, critically read, and interpret what the role actually needs. This is your job.
⚠️
JD vs Job Requisition (Req) — They Are NOT the Same
Job Description (JD):
Describes the role — skills, responsibilities, qualifications.
Job Requisition (Req):
The business/financial approval to hire. Contains: client name, req number, budget, timeline, bill rate.

When a job order arrives in the VMS, it contains BOTH. Read the req header first — confirm title, location, duration, and rate — before spending time analyzing the JD.

The Two Versions in Ceipal You Need to Know
📋
Original Job Description
The JD received from the client or MSP as-is. May contain client-identifying information, internal comments, garbled formatting, and HRIS export artifacts. Never share this with candidates.

✅
Posting Job Description
Cleaned, formatted version — with client identifiers removed. Can be pushed to job boards and your company's Jobs page. Always use this version when sharing JDs with candidates via email.

2
Caution
Do Not Trust the JD Blindly
Three things can make a JD misleading before it even reaches you. Spot these early — before you spend sourcing time chasing the wrong criteria.

🔠
Caution 1: Poorly Formatted
Bullet points become paragraph walls. Special characters turn into symbols. Hiring manager comments get left in the body. The original structure disappears in the HRIS export.

→ Flag: Rebuild the structure yourself before analyzing.
💬
Caution 2: Fluffy Language
Precise technical language gets replaced by corporate jargon during HR/legal review. "Must be able to query production databases" becomes "Strong analytical skills."

→ Flag: Read through jargon. Ask: what is the actual technical task?
📈
Caution 3: Inflated Demands
The Pareto principle: 80% of the role's value comes from 20% of listed requirements. But committees add nice-to-haves alongside the 3 things that actually matter.

→ Flag: Verify deal-breakers with manager before sourcing.
📋 Caution 1 in Action: Before & After a Poorly Formatted JD
This is a real example of what a VMS export looks like — and how a recruiter should reconstruct it.

❌ What Arrives in the VMS (Raw Export)
Java Software Engineer ** REMOTE ** EST hours - Fintech ** CONTRACT 6 months W2 ** IMPORTANT NOTE FROM HIRING MGR: MUST HAVE SPRING BOOT - NO EXCEPTIONS** Responsibilities include but are not limited to: design and develop scalable microservices; collaborate with cross-functional teams; work in agile environment; own delivery of features end to end; other duties as assigned (note: team is 8 ppl). Requirements: 5+ yrs Java; Spring Boot 2+ yrs; REST API exp; Micro svcs; AWS nice to have (EC2 S3 Lambda); Docker/K8s preferred; CI/CD - Jenkins. Educ: BS CS or related preferred. EEO Statement: Pacer Group is an equal opportunity employer...
✅ What You Reconstruct (Recruiter's Version)
Role: Java Software Engineer · Remote (EST) · 6mo Contract W2

🔴 DEAL-BREAKER: Spring Boot (hiring manager confirmed — non-negotiable)

Core Skills:
• Java 5+ yrs
• Spring Boot 2+ yrs
• REST APIs
• Microservices

Nice-to-Have:
• AWS (EC2/S3/Lambda)
• Docker, Kubernetes
• Jenkins CI/CD

Team: Agile squad of 8
Education: BS CS preferred (not required)
Ignore: EEO statement — legal boilerplate
🔍
What to spot in a poorly formatted JD:
CAPS LOCK NOTES
— Hiring manager comments left in the body. Always read these — they reveal the real deal-breaker.
** symbols and dashes
— Formatting artifacts from the HRIS export. Strip them mentally; they carry no meaning.
"Other duties as assigned"
— Ignore for sourcing. Legal placeholder, not a skill requirement.
💬 Caution 2 in Action: Translating Corporate Jargon
The rule: If you could put the phrase on any resume for any industry — it is fluff. If you can't put it in a Boolean string — it's not a sourcing criterion.

📄 JD Says (Fluffy)
"Strong analytical and problem-solving skills"
✅ What It Actually Means
Can read and write SQL queries, debug production data pipelines, and identify root causes of system failures under time pressure.
💡 Why
'Analytical skills' means nothing. Look at the tools section — SQL and monitoring tools tell you the real requirement.
📄 JD Says (Fluffy)
"Excellent communication skills required"
✅ What It Actually Means
Must present technical findings to non-technical stakeholders weekly; write clear engineering documentation for a cross-functional team.
💡 Why
Communication is listed in 90% of JDs. What matters is: who are they communicating with, and in what format?
📄 JD Says (Fluffy)
"Ability to work in a fast-paced, dynamic environment"
✅ What It Actually Means
Agile sprint cycle with daily standups; expected to context-switch between 2–3 features simultaneously; some weekend deployment support.
💡 Why
"Fast-paced" often means understaffed. Ask your manager: what does it mean specifically for this team?
📄 JD Says (Fluffy)
"Self-starter with a passion for technology"
✅ What It Actually Means
Works independently with minimal direction; expected to own features end-to-end without handholding from senior engineers.
💡 Why
Never put 'self-starter' in a Boolean string. Assess this on the screening call — not during sourcing.
📊 Caution 3 in Action: The 80/20 Priority Rule
How to apply: Count requirements (>8 = assume inflation), look for repetition, look for specificity, check for CAPS LOCK notes, then confirm with manager.

Priority	Item from the Java Engineer JD	Why This Priority	Sourcing Action
🔴 Deal-breaker	Spring Boot 2+ years	Hiring manager stated "NO EXCEPTIONS" in CAPS LOCK	Screen for this in the first 2 minutes of every call
🔵 Core	Java 11+ — 5+ years	Appears in overview, responsibilities, AND requirements = genuinely important	Boolean keyword. Immediate disqualification if missing.
🔵 Core	RESTful API design	Specific and repeated. Required to perform the job.	Boolean keyword + screening question
🔵 Core	Microservices architecture	Specific and repeated. Required to perform the job.	Boolean keyword + screening question
🟢 Nice-to-have	AWS (EC2, S3, Lambda)	Listed as "nice to have" in original. Genuine preference, not a deal-breaker.	OR group in Boolean. Don't filter out candidates who lack this.
🟢 Nice-to-have	Docker / Kubernetes, Jenkins CI/CD	Listed as "preferred" — can be learned on the job	OR group in Boolean. Positive signal, not a requirement.
⬜ Ignore	Bachelor's degree in CS or related	Listed as "preferred" — client will waive for strong candidates	Clarify with manager. Do not screen out on education alone.
⬜ Ignore	Excellent communication skills, Other duties as assigned	Boilerplate. Not searchable. Not a disqualifier.	Assess on call — never Boolean.
