Break It Down
The 14 Fields to Identify & Clarify
Before analysis, extract every field from the JD. If a field is blank or ambiguous — flag it to your manager. Never source blind.

🚫
The Golden Rule
Blank location + no confirmed rate + unclear shift = you cannot screen a single candidate properly. Always fill these gaps first, before opening any job board.

📋 View All 14 Fields with Guidance
▼
#	Field	Why It Matters to You as a Recruiter	If Missing — Ask Your Manager
01	Client	Industry context, culture, and household name factor. Look up the client before sourcing.	Is this confidential? What can I tell candidates without naming them?
02	Title	Drives Boolean title variations. "Software Engineer II" = "Mid-level Developer", "Java Developer", etc.	Is there a level (junior/mid/senior)? Alternative titles the hiring manager uses?
03	Location	Commute radius for onsite; time zone for remote. Compact licence relevance for healthcare.	Onsite, hybrid, or fully remote? If hybrid — how many days on-site?
04	Duration	Governs urgency, logistics, and candidate commitment. 3-month vs 12-month attracts different people.	Extension or conversion to perm possible? Client's intent?
05	Pay / Rate	Your negotiation anchor. Know the max pay before your first sourcing call. Never source blind on rate.	What is the bill rate and the margin floor?
06	Shift Timing	Filters availability. Night-shift ICU list differs from day-shift. Remote: time zone alignment matters.	Core hours? Any standby or on-call requirements?
07	Temp to Perm	Changes the conversation. Contract-to-hire opens passive candidates who'd decline a pure contract.	Defined conversion path? Trial period length and trigger conditions?
08	Hard Skills	Your PRIMARY sourcing criteria — the MUST HAVEs. Missing a key hard skill = immediate disqualification.	Which are deal-breakers vs preferred? Minimum experience level per skill?
09	Soft Skills	Culture fit indicators. Used in screening, not sourcing. You cannot Boolean-search for "self-starter."	What does team culture look like? How does the hiring manager describe ideal fit?
10	Tools	Version-specific and often NON-SUBSTITUTABLE. AWS ≠ Azure. Confirm specific tools before sourcing.	Required or preferred? Is training provided on proprietary tools?
11	Certifications	Mandatory in many fields. Missing a required cert = automatic rejection at submission.	Required day 1 or can it be in progress? Which body issues it?
12	Education	Often aspirational. Clients say "degree required" but waive it for strong hands-on experience.	Equivalent experience acceptable? Minimum they'll actually consider?
13	Deal-breakers	The 1–3 requirements causing automatic rejection. Confirm before sourcing — saves hours.	Of all requirements listed, which 3 cause immediate rejection?
14	Missing Info	Blank location, rate, or shift = you cannot screen properly. Always fill gaps first.	Flag ALL blanks before sourcing. Do not source blind — period.
📝 JD Reading Strategy: Three Passes
⚡
Pass 1 — Quick (2 min)
Skim through without stopping. Get mental notes of what sections are present: overview? responsibilities? tools? certifications? Don't read — survey.

🔬
Pass 2 — Detailed (5–7 min)
Read every sentence. Next to each, write a 5-word-or-less remark: "core task," "vague fluff," "specific tool," "legal padding."

🎯
Pass 3 — Highlight (3 min)
Bold the required skills and tools. Italicize preferred/nice-to-have skills. Now you have a clean sourcing map.

4
Classification
Hard Skill vs Soft Skill vs Tool
These three categories drive three completely different parts of your process. Confusing them is one of the most common mistakes freshers make.

🧠
The Simplest Test
If it's something you do (a verb-based capability) →
Skill
(Hard or Soft)
If it's a system, software, platform, or certification →
Tool / Technology
If you can Boolean-search for it and get useful results →
Hard Skill or Tool
If it applies to literally any job in any industry →
Soft Skill — screen for it, don't source it
🔴 Hard Skill
Definition
A teachable, measurable technical ability. Either a candidate has it or they don't.
Recruiter Use
PRIMARY sourcing criteria. Boolean-searchable. Missing a key hard skill = immediate disqualification.
Examples
Java 11 / Python / SQL
Spring Boot / REST APIs
Microservices architecture
ICD-10 coding / Data modelling
"Can you walk me through a project where you built a RESTful API using Spring Boot?"
Watch Out
"Good at coding" — not a hard skill. That's a description of having hard skills.
🔵 Soft Skill
Definition
An interpersonal or behavioural attribute. Difficult to measure objectively.
Recruiter Use
Used for culture fit assessment — not sourcing. You cannot Boolean-search for soft skills.
Examples
Communication
Collaboration / Leadership
Attention to detail
Adaptability / Time management
"Tell me about a time you communicated a complex technical issue to a non-technical stakeholder."
Watch Out
Never put "self-starter" or "excellent communicator" in a Boolean string. Zero useful results.
🟢 Tool
Definition
A specific software, platform, machine, or technology used in the role. External to the person.
Recruiter Use
Version-specific and often NON-SUBSTITUTABLE. AWS ≠ Azure. Confirm before sourcing.
Examples
JIRA / Confluence / Salesforce
GitHub / Kubernetes / Tableau
Epic (EMR) / Cerner / SAP
Jenkins / Docker / ServiceNow
"You've listed Kubernetes — have you managed production clusters, or dev/staging environments?"
Watch Out
"Experience with enterprise software" is not a tool reference. Ask the hiring manager what specific tools the team uses.
5
Analysis
The 4-Step Analysis Framework
After you've broken down the JD, you analyse it. This is what transforms you from a keyword-matcher into a recruiter who actually understands the role.

🏢
Step 1 — Client Context
What does the client do, and how does this role help them do it? A recruiter who understands the business can pitch the role in the language of impact — not just job duties.

🔍
Step 2 — The Problem Being Solved
Why does this req exist? Every open role exists because something is missing. Knowing what it is shapes your candidate pitch and tells you the urgency level.

🎬
Step 3 — Narrator Exercise
Close the JD and write 3–5 sentences describing a day in the life of this person. If you can narrate it, you understand the role well enough to find the right candidate.

📊
Step 4 — TSTE Table
Build the Title/Skills/Tools/Education table before opening any job board. This is the bridge from JD analysis to Boolean search.

Step 2 — The 4 Types of Req (Know Your Urgency Level)
Req Type	What It Means	Urgency	Candidate Pitch Angle
Growth Req	Client is expanding — needs more hands	Lower	"Join a growing team" — good for candidates considering stability
Backfill Req	Someone left — work is piling up	High — need yesterday	Emphasize immediate impact. Candidate should ask why the person left.
New Capability	Client needs a skill they don't have internally	Urgent AND specific	Emphasize uniqueness. No one on the team can do what they're being hired to do.
Project Req	Short-term need for a specific project phase	Deadline-driven	Clear scope, defined timeline — good for contractors preferring project-based work
Step 3 — The Narrator Exercise: 7 Questions to Answer
Close the JD. Write 3–5 sentences describing a typical day. If you can't answer any of these 7 questions — go back to the JD. If the JD doesn't answer it — ask your manager.

Q1
What kind of office/environment does this person work in? (Startup? Enterprise HQ? Remote? Hospital unit?)
Q2
How does their day start? (Standup? Email review? Production monitoring? Patient rounds?)
Q3
In what sequence do they perform their core tasks? (Design → Code → Review → Deploy? Or reactive/interrupt-driven?)
Q4
Where and how do the tools appear in their work? (Is AWS used for deployment? Is Spring Boot the framework they build in or maintain?)
Q5
What soft skills are actually in play? (Do they present to stakeholders? Lead a team? Coordinate across time zones?)
Q6
Do they manage or mentor others? (IC contributor vs tech lead — very different candidate profiles)
Q7
Who do they report to and how? (Flat startup → direct to CTO? Large enterprise → multiple layers of managers?)
✍️ Worked Example — Narrator for: Java Software Engineer, Fintech Payments Platform, Remote
Alex is a mid-to-senior Java engineer working remotely from EST hours. Their day starts with a 9am standup with an 8-person agile squad — a quick sync on sprint progress, blockers, and deployment status. The team runs 2-week sprints, so Alex has a clear delivery cadence.

The core of their day is feature development: building microservices in Java using the Spring Boot framework — specifically the real-time payment processing module. They write REST API endpoints, test them, and integrate with the existing payment infrastructure. Most of their code runs in AWS (Lambda for event-driven functions, EC2 for services, S3 for storage). They use Jenkins for CI/CD.

Alex owns features end-to-end. This is not a ticket-and-hand-off team — they design, build, test, and deploy. High autonomy. They report to a senior engineering manager but work independently. Communication happens mostly async via Slack and Confluence documentation.

EST remote = time zone matters for sourcing geography
Spring Boot = the deal-breaker skill in action
8-person squad = collaboration without management responsibility
High autonomy = soft skill: self-starter (assess on call, not Boolean)
End-to-end ownership = IC contributor, not a cog in a machine
'Seen production Spring Boot' = your first screening question
