Glossary
Key Terms — Quick Reference
Use this when you encounter any of these terms in training, in the VMS, or on the job.

JD (Job Description)
Document describing a role's responsibilities, skills, tools, qualifications. Starting point — not final truth. Created by hiring manager, modified by HR and legal.
Job Requisition (Req)
Business/financial approval to hire. Contains client name, req number, budget, timeline, and bill rate. NOT the same as a JD. Read the req header before the JD.
VMS (Vendor Management System)
Software platform clients use to manage their staffing vendors and post job orders. Job orders arrive here. Examples: Beeline, Fieldglass, Coupa, Wand.
ATS (Applicant Tracking System)
Your internal database for tracking candidates and submissions. Ceipal is your ATS at Pacer Group.
HRIS
Human Resources Information System. Client's internal HR software (e.g., Workday, SAP). JDs are often exported from HRIS — causing formatting loss.
Deal-breaker
A requirement whose absence causes automatic rejection of a candidate at the client submission or interview stage. Confirm with manager before sourcing. Typically 1–3 per req.
Hard Skill
Teachable, measurable technical ability. Either a candidate has it or they don't. PRIMARY sourcing criteria. Boolean-searchable. Examples: Java, SQL, Spring Boot, ICD-10 coding.
Soft Skill
Interpersonal/behavioural attribute. Cannot be Boolean-searched. Assessed on the screening call. Examples: communication, leadership, adaptability, attention to detail.
Tool
External software, platform, or technology used in the role. Version-specific. Often non-substitutable (AWS ≠ Azure). Examples: JIRA, Tableau, Epic EMR, Jenkins, Cerner.
Boolean Search
Search technique using AND/OR/NOT operators to find candidates. Built from the TSTE table. Covered in your next module (Topic 3). Example: ("Java Developer" OR "Java Engineer") AND "Spring Boot"
TSTE Table
Pre-Boolean analysis framework: Title variations, Skills (required), Tools (required), Education/Experience + Preferred skills + Research alternatives. Built before opening any job board.
Narrator Exercise
Technique where the recruiter closes the JD and writes 3–5 sentences describing a typical day in the role. Answers 7 specific questions. Proves role understanding before sourcing.
80/20 Rule (for JDs)
80% of the role's value comes from 20% of the listed requirements. Used to identify deal-breakers vs. noise when a JD has too many listed requirements.
Original JD vs Posting JD
Original = as received from client (may contain internal info, garbled format). Posting = cleaned, formatted, client info removed. Always share Posting JD with candidates.
EEO Statement
Equal Employment Opportunity legal disclaimer found at the end of most JDs. Boilerplate. Ignore for sourcing — adds zero candidate criteria.
HIPAA
Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act. US law governing patient data privacy. A non-negotiable compliance requirement for all healthcare data roles. Awareness is often a deal-breaker for those roles.
